prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a dual-portal rendering effect—each portal displays a view of the scene as seen from the location of the other portal, using additional cameras and render-target techniques.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import through ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Room Scene]
(1) One large room: BoxGeometry(30, 10, 30), inward-facing (side: THREE.BackSide), divided into left and right halves;
(2) The left walls use a bluish color scheme, the right walls use a reddish color scheme, the floor is gray, and the ceiling is light gray;
(3) Place several colored marker objects in the scene for spatial orientation:
  - Left area: 3 blue/cyan cubes, randomly placed at different positions
  - Right area: 3 red/orange spheres, randomly placed at different positions
  - Center: 1 large yellow cylinder
(4) Ambient light + one PointLight in each area (blue on the left, red on the right);

[Portal Frames]
(5) Portal 1: located on the inner side of the left wall (x ≈ -14), facing the +x direction;
(6) Portal 2: located on the inner side of the right wall (x ≈ +14), facing the -x direction;
(7) Each portal consists of two parts:
  - Frame: TorusGeometry(portalSize, 0.3, 16, 64), with a glowing material (MeshStandardMaterial with an emissive color; Portal 1 blue #0088ff, Portal 2 orange #ff4400);
  - Portal surface: CircleGeometry(portalSize) or PlaneGeometry(portalSize*2, portalSize*2), using MeshBasicMaterial({map: renderTarget.texture}), with its front side facing into the room;
(8) The default value of portalSize is 2, controlled by the slider with id="portalSize" (range 1-4, step 0.1);

[Render-to-Texture Portal Views]
(9) Two additional PerspectiveCamera instances: portalCamera1 and portalCamera2;
(10) Two WebGLRenderTarget instances: renderTarget1 and renderTarget2 (resolution 512×512 or 1024×1024);
(11) Rendering logic (executed every frame):
  - Calculate the transform of portalCamera1: transform the main camera's position/direction relative to Portal 2 into Portal 1's coordinate system (simulating that “looking out from Portal 1 shows the viewpoint at Portal 2”);
  - Specific algorithm: portalCamera1.position = portal1.worldMatrix * inverse(portal2.worldMatrix) * mainCamera.position; transform portalCamera1.quaternion in the same way;
  - Calculate portalCamera2 in the same way;
  - Render the portalCamera1 view to renderTarget1 and map it onto the surface of Portal 1;
  - Render the portalCamera2 view to renderTarget2 and map it onto the surface of Portal 2;
(12) When rendering a portal view, hide the corresponding portal-surface mesh (to avoid recursive rendering issues);

[Stencil Buffer Mask (Optional but Recommended)]
(13) Use the stencil buffer to restrict the portal view to the shape inside the portal frame (producing clean portal edges). If stencil is not used, at minimum use alphaTest or clipping to ensure that the portal view is visible only within the portal-surface area;

[Control Panel]
(14) Portal toggle with id="portalToggle" (checkbox): when unchecked, the portal surfaces become solid-color opaque materials (the portal views are no longer rendered); when checked, restore them;
(15) Portal-size slider with id="portalSize", range 1-4, step 0.1, default 2; when adjusted, scale the frames and portal surfaces synchronously;

[Camera Controls]
(16) OrbitControls, with target pointing at the center of the room (0, 2, 0);
(17) Initial position: a viewpoint from which both portals and the central object are visible (for example, (0, 5, 12));

[State Exposure (Required)]
(18) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame, containing:
  - portalEnabled: boolean (whether the portals are enabled)
  - portalSize: number (portal-size parameter)
  - cameraPos: { x: number, y: number, z: number } (main camera position)
  - portalPositions: [{ x: number, y: number, z: number }, { x: number, y: number, z: number }] (world coordinates of the two portals)
  - fps: number (average frame rate over the most recent 1 second)

Output a single HTML file that can run when opened directly in a browser.
